/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  landscape03_steam_reforming
  ===========================

  THE REFORMING LANDSCAPE -- CH4 + 3 H2O (gibbs02_steam_reforming's own feed
  and species set), the fraction of the feed carbon STILL in methane against
  temperature at 1, 10 and 100 bar.  Read it as 1 minus the methane
  conversion: LESS is MORE converted, and the page labels it so rather than
  subtracting -- the metric on the axis is exactly the number the engine
  wrote.  (`elementYield` with CH4 as the "product" is the honest way to ask
  for the unconverted feed; the metric slot accepts any listed species.)

  WHAT THE THREE CURVES SHOW.  Endothermic (the slip falls as T rises) and
  mole-INCREASING (CH4 + H2O -> CO + 3 H2, 2 -> 4), so pressure HURTS: the
  100 bar curve sits far above the 1 bar one, which is why industrial
  reformers run hot and why the shift on the same catalyst (exothermic,
  Delta_n = 0) is the one the approach caveat calls GLOBAL -- one dT for
  two reactions of opposite thermicity.  The engine reads the OVERALL
  thermicity from this feed (endothermic at every cell of this grid, the
  run's own tally) and evaluates the chemistry at T - dT.

  WHY THE PRESSURES ARE 1, 10 AND 100 BAR.  `Pgrid` is `from / to / n / log`
  (GibbsMapOp.cpp reads no list form), and three log-spaced points from 1 to
  100 bar land on the decades a textbook draws.  Ideal-gas validity is the
  same caveat as nh3_equilibrium_map's: the high-pressure curve is the
  SHAPE of the pressure dependence, not a quantitative prediction.

  temperatureApproach IS DECLARED, AT ZERO.  The EduTool's slider replaces
  the NUMBER of a declared scalar and can add no key (gui/src/case/
  methodRun.ts), so the key must be here for the page to move it.  Zero is
  true equilibrium and prints nothing; a magnitude > 0 makes the run assign
  the sign at EVERY cell from the thermicity of the overall transformation
  at that cell's T (GibbsMapOp.cpp, `resolveDirection`) and print the tally.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

operations
(
    {
        name      reformer;
        type      gibbsMap;
        elements  ( C H O );
        species
        (
            { name CH4;   atoms ( 1 4 0 ); }
            { name water; atoms ( 0 2 1 ); }
            { name CO;    atoms ( 1 0 1 ); }
            { name CO2;   atoms ( 1 0 2 ); }
            { name H2;    atoms ( 0 2 0 ); }
        );
        feed      { CH4 1.0;  water 3.0; }
        Tgrid     { from 600; to 1300; n 36; }             // K
        Pgrid     { from 1.0e5; to 1.0e7; n 3; log true; }  // Pa: 1, 10, 100 bar
        temperatureApproach 0;                          // K, a MAGNITUDE; 0 = true equilibrium
        metric    { type elementYield; element C; product CH4; }
        anchors
        (
            { T 1000;     P 1.0e5;   }   // gibbs02's operating point
        );
        output    { file map.csv; }
    }
);
